<$transclude $tiddler="$:/plugins/rimir/doc-template/templates/page">
<$fill $name="body">

!! Approach

The tiddler is rendered unchanged. A JS widget, `$outline`, wraps the normal body transclusion, walks the rendered widget tree and stamps `data-ol-*` attributes on the DOM node of each unit. One delegated `keydown` and `click` handler lives on the widget's container. Operations are splices of known text ranges. Wrapper elements inside the body are avoided on purpose: they would change the HTML and break CSS such as `ul > li`. The one exception is the widget's own container: while outline mode is on, the whole body sits inside `div.rr-ol-frame > div.rr-ol` (the frame also holds the key overview), so selectors such as `.tc-tiddler-body > p` no longer match (use `.tc-tiddler-body p` or also match `.tc-tiddler-body > .rr-ol-frame > .rr-ol > p`).

!! Parts

|!File |!Role |
|`model.js` |Collects units from the widget tree: start, end, kind, level, parent, siblings |
|`text-utils.js`, `dom-utils.js` |Pure text helpers; DOM lookup shared by widget and editor |
|`formats.js` |Registry of format adapters |
|`format-wikitext.js` |Wikitext adapter: indent, promote, move (siblings, and across heading lines), replace, insertNew, insertBlock (after / before / at the end), convert, remove, deleteUnit, merge, split, editSource, isMultiline |
|`outline-widget.js` |The `$outline` widget: unit-to-DOM mapping, stamps, selection, writes |
|`outline-keys.js` |Key dispatch through `$:/config/shortcuts/outline-*`; toggles the key overview |
|`keys/*`, `ui/keys-table`, `keys.tid` |Key groups (caption, hint, list of shortcut names), the overview table, the keys tab |
|`outline-editor.js` |In-place editor: temp tiddler, placeholder, save, continue, conflict handling |
|`outline-menu.js` |Menus at the selection: block menu (Shift-Enter: insert / convert / edit) and delete confirmation (Delete); keyboard and mouse |
|`outline-clipboard.js` |The browser's copy / cut / paste events on the focused outline: the adapter's `copy` gives `{kind, source}`, written as the outline format, as plain text (source) and as HTML (source rendered, tiddler links as text); cut is copy plus `deleteUnit`; paste reads the outline format or plain text (adapter `clipKind`), `paste` parses the clip with the model and rewrites only its item markers / heading levels for the place, one write |
|`outline-undo.js` |Undo/redo: every `writeText` records the text before and after plus the selection in `$:/temp/rimir/outline/undo/<title>`; `undoGroup` makes the save and the operation of a split, merge or editor move one step; undo writes only over the text it left |
|`language/blocks.multids` |Labels of the block types and menu groups |
|`ui/*` |Toolbar button and icon, body template and its filter, help bar (? button and overview), editor template, transclusion marker |
|`styles.tid` |Selection frame and item box, compact editor, marker |

!! Routing

`ui/toolbar-button` toggles `$:/state/rimir/outline/on/<title>` (`yes`/`no`). `ui/body-filter` (a `$:/tags/ViewTemplateBodyFilter` entry) routes eligible tiddlers with state `yes` to `ui/body`, which renders `<$outline>` around the usual block transclusion, plus the ? button and, while `$:/temp/rimir/outline/help/<title>` is `yes`, the key overview. Both sit outside `<$outline>`, so they never become units; the widget only sets and deletes that temp tiddler (`?`, Escape).

!! Editor

The editor is a real TW edit widget bound to a temp tiddler (`$:/temp/rimir/outline/...`), placed where the unit was. It lives outside the rendered body's widget tree, so a re-render of the body does not destroy it. Typing changes only the temp tiddler; the real tiddler changes once per save.

The buffer holds the unit's content only: an item's marker and a heading's `!`s (plus the spaces after them) stay in the tiddler and are not part of the edit (`editSource`/`replace` in the adapter). `ui/editor` renders a compact textarea (no toolbar, SimpleEngine) or, with `$:/config/rimir/outline/full-editor` = `yes`, the full text editor with toolbar.

!! Block menu

Shift-Enter in selection mode opens `.rr-ol-menu`, absolutely positioned below the selected unit in `.rr-ol`. Its entries: //Insert after// (every block type; not for a single list item) and //Turn into// (the adapter's `convertTypes`). Insert opens a new-unit editor with a `blockType` (kept in the buffer field `outline-block-type`); saving calls the adapter's `insertBlock`, which puts a new heading after the anchor heading's whole section and any other block right after the heading line (`insertsAfterSubtree`; the editor is placed accordingly). Turn into runs `operate("convert", type)`: a bullet switches its marker character with all its siblings of that level (and their sub-items at that marker position), a whole list at all levels; paragraphs, headings and code blocks are rewritten from their content and set apart by blank lines. `convert` parses its result again and refuses it unless the new unit is where the old one was and the unit count changed only by an added list unit.

!! Selection frame

Units get the class `rr-ol-selected`. A list item's element contains its sub-items, so for an item with sub-items the frame is an overlay box (`.rr-ol-selbox`, absolutely positioned in `.rr-ol`) around the item's own lines, measured with a DOM range up to the nested list. It is redrawn on every selection change and when the outline container resizes (ResizeObserver).

A re-render of an ancestor (for example a changed `$:/tags/Global` or `$:/tags/Macro` tiddler re-renders the page template) or closing the tiddler destroys the `$outline` widget. A buffer that differs from what the editor started with is then kept: it carries `outline-orig-source`, `outline-orig-start`, `outline-orig-text`, `outline-is-new` and `outline-unit-kind`, and the next `$outline` widget of the tiddler reopens the editor at the unit, located as on save (or with the conflict notice when the unit is gone). Escape and switching outline mode off discard it.

!! Writes

A single `wiki.addTiddler` of the old tiddler plus the new `text` and modification fields. Before writing, the text must equal the text the positions were computed from, otherwise the view re-renders first. If the text is this view's own last write (a key pressed before the refresh after a write), the operation goes on on the rebuilt mapping; after any other change nothing is written.

Structural operations pass a model guard (`checkOp` in `model.js`) before they are written: the result is parsed again, and a move must keep the unit count and the kinds and levels; indent/promote must keep the unit count and change only the operated subtree's levels, by exactly one. Anything else is refused without a write. Structural keys do nothing while the view has an open editor.

